 ## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
 
-- RLS on every new or changed table, comparing `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text` and scoping by company; 
API routes resolve the application user by `supabase_uid`.
-- API keys stored encrypted at rest, readable only by server code, never returned (only a mask), never logged.
-- Rich text sanitized on write and render; Markdown export escapes content so it cannot inject HTML when rendered 
elsewhere.
-- Transcript text is sent to a third-party provider only after the notice naming that provider; the admin's provider 
choice (including providers hosted outside Brazil/US) is the company's decision.
-- Prompt-injection resistance: transcript is passed as data; output parsed against a fixed schema; any field outside 
the schema is discarded.
-- Abuse: daily limit per company; size limits; server-side validation of every limit.
+- RLS on every new or changed table, comparing `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text` and scoping by the note's 
stored `company_id`; API routes resolve the application user by `supabase_uid`; authorization uses the pre-change 
state.
+- Column-level protection: completion-only path for assignees; immutable `company_id`, `created_by`, `note_id`, 
creation times; versions are insert-only.
+- API keys encrypted at rest in server-only storage (no authenticated/anon grants, no Realtime publication), 
decrypted only inside server code for a dispatch, never returned, never logged; provider error bodies sanitized.
+- Rich text sanitized on write and render; AI output treated as untrusted; Markdown export escapes HTML.
+- Transcript text goes to a third-party provider only after the notice naming that provider for the current settings 
revision; the admin's provider choice (including providers hosted outside Brazil/US) is the company's decision.
+- Abuse and cost: daily limit counted at dispatch with atomic reservations and idempotency; size and context 
preflight; server-side validation of every limit.
 - Subscription credentials (Claude, ChatGPT) are never requested or stored.
 
