Leaving the page cancels the browser request; the server makes a best-effort abort of the outbound provider request 
and discards the draft; counting follows FR-022 regardless of whether the abort succeeds. Before returning a draft, 
the server rechecks that the caller is still a member of the note's company and the note still exists; otherwise the 
draft is discarded. A discarded or cancelled operation keeps no draft content; its replay returns the terminal status 
"discarded" without dispatch. In the browser, drafts and note data are scoped to the signed-in application user and 
company; on sign-out, removal from the company, or company change, pending requests are cancelled, drafts and cached 
notes are cleared, and late responses for the previous scope are dropped. | Must | AC-016, AC-030, AC-033 |
123:| FR-022 | Usage: each provider dispatch counts toward the company's daily limit, whether it succeeds, times out, 
returns unusable output, or the user leaves. A dispatch is refunded only when the provider's initial HTTP response is 
a non-2xx rejection (with its normal error body) whose status/type/code is listed for that adapter in the capability 
matrix as authentication/permission, rate-limit, overload, or unavailable; errors inside an accepted (2xx or 
streaming) response, unknown statuses or codes, and connection loss after the request was sent stay counted. Not 
counted at all: files rejected by FR-013, inputs refused by FR-020, failures before dispatch. Admission atomically 
reserves one unit against the limit before dispatch; concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit; each reservation 
covers exactly one dispatch and settles at most once against the day of its admission; a reservation without a 
